source: ASF Jira Server 8.20.10, POST /rest/api/1.0/render (hack/jira-render-evidence.py), captured 2026-08-24
observed: A `|` one backslash byte stands in front of separates no cells, and the run in front of this one is two backslashes long: Jira renders the two data cells `a` with a forced newline and `|b`, and `c`. A rendered cell value ending in a backslash would merge its cell with the next one the same way, so jiro writes none.
-- input.jira --
||h||
|a\\|b|c|
-- rendered.html --
<div class='table-wrap'>
<table class='confluenceTable'><tbody>
<tr>
<th class='confluenceTh'>h</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class='confluenceTd'>a<br class="atl-forced-newline" />|b</td>
<td class='confluenceTd'>c</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
-- want.md --
:::table
||h||
|a\\|b|c|
:::
-- warnings.json --
[]
